#import "../../lib/24xx.typ": *

#show: xx.with(
  title: "AX&X: The Alehouse",
  subtitle: "A dockside common room of porters, sailors, and travelers: carousing, the clear-out cup, the brawl, and the rumors in the ale. An alehouse supplement for AX&X.",
  cols: 2,
)

= Between Delves

_Burned into the beam over the bar: no hittin', no spittin', no fightin', no killin'. By last call, all four are broken._

Every delve begins and ends in the same place: a low room, a fire, and a cup. This one is a #k[dockside alehouse], where the river stairs empty at dusk and porters, sailors, and travelers wash up with coin to spend and news to tell. It is where a party hears its next job, spends its last, hires its spare hands, and makes the trouble that becomes the next adventure. This adds no new dice. A night out runs on #k[Checks], #k[Reaction], and #k[Morale], the same as any other scene, and most of it is talk. Roll only when the night turns on something: a contest, a brawl, a boast that someone decides to test.

The coin you carry home is already your reward, #k[1 GP = 1 XP] once it reaches safety. What follows is how a party spends it, and what the spending costs.

= The House

- #item[Bed and Board][A night's rest the game needs: a common-room floor #k[1 SP], a shared bed #k[5 SP], a private room #k[1 GP], each with a hot meal thrown in. A #k[week's board] is the town leg of a #k[Haven], where HP returns and Injured mends.]
- #item[Stable and Store][Stabling and feed for a mount run a few SP a night. Most inns keep a #k[strongbox] for a fee, safer than a pack left in a room, and a trusted keeper will mind a party's coin or hold a letter against their return.]
- #item[A Quiet Word][The right keeper fences what a party would rather not carry to the open market, at a cut, and knows who buys the rest. Ask with a #k[CHA Check], and a #k[Reaction] sets the price of discretion.]

= Carousing

- #item[A Night's Revel][Between delves, blow your winnings on the town. A lavish night costs a #k[tenth of your haul or 50 GP], whichever is more, and buys a roll on #k[The Morning After]: a rumor earned, a friend made, or a mess to wake to. A party may carouse together on one shared spend.]
- #item[The Bigger the Spend][Great wealth thrown around draws great trouble. Add #k[+1 to the Morning After roll] for each further #k[50 GP] spent past the first, and the GM reads the night from where it lands.]
- #item[Drink and Dice][Alehouses run games, too: a wager on knucklebones or the turn of a card is a #k[CHA or INT Check] against the house, 12+, stake for stake. The house cheats when it can.]

= The Clear-Out Cup

The house is clearing old stock the only way a crowd will swallow it: a contest. Most empty cups before you at the last bell takes the pot.

- #item[The Pot][Most empty cups wins. The house pours its oldest casks, so #k[one cup in three has turned] (1d6: 1--2), telegraphed by a sour reek and an oily sheen. The stock runs #k[six pours] or so, and then the bell.]
- #item[A Turn at the Bench][Keep it loose. Trade boasts and toasts freely, and only call a turn when someone #k[does a thing that needs a roll]: drinks, smells a cup, tips one out, or works a rival.]
- #item[Holding Your Drink][A cup #k[drains 1 point of CON], the night's toll, restored by a night's rest. On drinking, make a #k[Body save at your current CON mod], 12+. Fail and the cup #k[does not count], slopped down your chin, and you #k[lurch]: Weakened on your next action. #k[Three lurches, or CON 3], and you slide under the bench: Helpless, your cups frozen where they sit.]
- #item[The Four Roads][The pot is #k[relative]: most cups wins, so tearing a rival down serves as well as drinking. #k[Fortitude:] drink, and take the save. #k[Wits:] read the cups, or pace yourself so the next lurch cannot land. #k[Guile] and #k[Charm] never drink; they cheat and sabotage.]
- #item[Cheat and Sabotage][#k[Ditch a cup] (Guile, DEX or a Thief's Class Check, #k[9+], easy sleight in a packed, loud room): tip it out a window, behind the settle, or into a neighbor's pack, and it counts #k[empty], no drink; caught, you forfeit #k[1 cup]. #k[Spill a rival's] cup (Guile, 12+) and the house blames them and takes their #k[2 cups], but miss and the 2 is yours. #k[Foist a turned cup] (Charm, 12+): the rival drinks it, and a failed sour save #k[scores them nothing] and lands a lurch. #k[Start a brawl] with a taunt or a hidden strike pinned on another, and a rival drawn in is #k[thrown out], gone from the count; fail and the bouncer takes you instead.]
- #item[The Turned Cup][Spotting one is no sure thing: a #k[Wits (INT) Check], 12+ by the sour reek, or 15+ if it has been doctored under a lime, or read only by a #k[finger-dip] (the sour and the cheap taste much alike). A mouthful of the sour tests any gut: its save is a flat #k[15+, with CON no help], and it drains #k[2 CON]. A cleric's #k[Purify Food and Drink] makes a cup ordinary ale again, no help at all against the drink itself.]
- #item[The Bell][Most cups wins, counted only among those #k[still sitting upright]. A contestant face-down in the rushes has no claim, whatever stands before them.]

#rolltable("1d6", "At the Long Bench", note: [The house runs a ringer or two to drive the night. Roll or pick.], (
  item[Old Bessa, a dwarf stevedore][An iron gut and no guile: pure fortitude, setting a pace meant to drown the field. The one to beat.],
  item[Finch, a peddler][All sleight and no stomach. Tips every cup, frames rivals for the spill, and never seems to drink.],
  item[Mad Gurt, a bargeman][Drinks everything, turned cups and all, and roars. Crashes out early and loud, often taking a neighbor down with them.],
  item[A river pilgrim][Quietly purifies the worst cups and sips slow. Outlasts the brawlers by never once lurching.],
  item[A young sailor][Loud, proud, and easily steered: a Charm check lands the turned cups square in their hand.],
  item[The house itself][The tapster nudges the stock, so the turned cups tend to reach whoever is winning. Fair is not the point; clearing the cellar is.],
))

= An Alehouse Brawl

- #item[Fists and Stools][A brawl is #k[nonlethal] by default. A fist or a grabbed stool deals #k[1d3], and a foe at 0 drops #k[Helpless], not slain, as the core sets out. A thrown mug reaches Near on a Check, a table is cover, and the chandelier is a Dodge save waiting for someone to swing on it.]
- #item[How It Starts][A spilled drink, a called cheat, a slur, a lost wager. When the fists come up, roll #k[Reaction] for the room: a hostile crowd piles in, a wary one rings the fighters and bets.]
- #item[How It Ends][#k[Morale] (1d6) when the first body hits the floor and again at half strength; the losers yield or scatter. A CHA Check can still buy calm. The bouncer, #k[HD 2] and a weighted cudgel, wades in to end what the house did not sell tickets for.]
- #item[When Steel Comes Out][Draw a blade and it is a fight like any other, lethal, and the #k[Watch] comes, #k[1d6] guardsmen with the law at their back. The room empties, and your face is remembered.]

= Hiring Hands

- #item[The Help][The alehouse is where the spare hands wait. A #k[porter or torchbearer] works for a few SP a day and no share of the danger; a #k[man-at-arms] wants #k[5 GP a day] and a cut of the take; a #k[guide or linkboy] knows the near country or the low streets.]
- #item[Will They Come][Offer the work and roll #k[Reaction] with CHA: a fair wage and an honest face hire easy, while a grim rumor or a miser's purse sends them back to their cups. The desperate and the green come cheapest, and cost the most later.]
- #item[Will They Stay][Hirelings test #k[Morale] when the delve turns deadly, the pay runs short, or the first of them falls. Pay square and treat them well, and the good ones become the #k[henchmen] a reputation is built on.]

#rolltable("1d8", "Who's In Tonight", note: [Roll when a party settles in, or when the night needs a push.], (
  item[A recruiting sergeant][Buying rounds for anyone who will take the coin and the colors with it.],
  item[A bard or pilgrim][Fresh off the road with news, a song, and a rumor to trade for a cup.],
  item[A hooded stranger][In the far corner, watching the door, with work that pays well and asks nothing.],
  item[A cutpurse][Working the crowd. A WIS check to feel the hand already at your belt.],
  item[An off-duty guard][Three cups in and loose-lipped about the watch, the walls, and the gate.],
  item[A drunk with a map][Half a treasure and half a tale, sold cheap, and lying in at least one place.],
  item[A rival party][Louder and richer, crowing about the very dungeon you had your eye on.],
  item[An old debt][A creditor, a bounty-man, or a mark from a past job, and they have just seen your face.],
))

#rolltable("1d8", "The Morning After", note: [Read the night from the roll, plus any spend over the first 50 GP.], (
  item[A clear head][And a solid lead: the GM hands you one true rumor, no strings.],
  item[A new friend][Sleeping it off in the corner, a willing hireling, Reaction already friendly.],
  item[A fuzzy mark][A tattoo, a shaved patch, or a marriage line you cannot account for.],
  item[A debt][You owe the house 2d6 x 10 GP, and the keeper knows your face and your inn.],
  item[The wrong bed][You wake a town over, in a cell, a cart, or a stranger's loft, gear scattered to find.],
  item[A challenge][Someone from the night wants satisfaction at dawn: a duel, a race, or a dare.],
  item[Lighter pockets][Robbed as you slept, 1d6 x 10 GP and one random item gone.],
  item[Marked trouble][Branded a brawler: the Watch wants a word, and this inn's door is shut to you.],
))

= On Tap

#table(
  columns: (auto, auto, 1fr),
  stroke: (y: 0.4pt + luma(80%)),
  inset: (x: 4pt, y: 3pt),
  table.header([Drink], [Cost], [Note]),
  [Small beer], [1 CP], [Waters the day. Too weak to drain CON; useless in the Cup.],
  [Ale, a tankard], [3 CP], [The common cup, and the contest's measure.],
  [Wine, a cup], [4 CP], [Dearer by the mile from the vineyards; a southern luxury up north.],
  [Firewater], [1 SP], [A measure drinks like two cups: 2 CON, a Body save each.],
  [The aged stock], [1 GP+], [Dwarven stout, elf-wine, a lord's reserve. A prize to win, a punishment to drink.],
)

A cup is a cup for the count, turned or not, strong or weak. What changes is what it does to you between the pour and the bell.

#rolltable("1d8", "What's the Occasion", note: [What the common room is hosting when the party walks in.], (
  item[An ordinary night][A low fire, low talk, and a keeper glad of the custom.],
  item[Market's end][The room is packed, loud, and flush with the day's coin.],
  item[A wake][Free ale and raw grief for a local, a mood that turns to a brawl on a word.],
  item[A name-day feast][Music, dancing, and a host generous enough to cover a round or three.],
  item[The eve of a hanging][Or a hunt at dawn. The crowd is grim and drinking to the bottom.],
  item[A holy day][Taps half-closed, the pious counting who drinks and how deep.],
  item[A fair][Traveling folk, games of chance, a prize on offer, and the Clear-Out Cup in full swing.],
  item[A press-gang][A recruiter holds the door and buys the drink, the better to wake the unwary in chains.],
))
